Spherical coordinates are $(\rho, \theta, \phi)$, and cylindrical coordinates are $(r, \theta, z)$.

Given: $\rho = 2$, $\theta = \frac{2\pi}{3}$, and $\phi = \frac{\pi}{6}$.

To convert from spherical to cylindrical, use the following formulas:
\begin{align*}
r &= \rho \sin \phi \\
\theta &= \theta \\
z &= \rho \cos \phi
\end{align*}

Substitute the given values:
\begin{align*}
r &= 2 \sin \frac{\pi}{6} = 2 \left( \frac{1}{2} \right) = 1 \\
\theta &= \frac{2\pi}{3} \\
z &= 2 \cos \frac{\pi}{6} = 2 \left( \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) = \sqrt{3}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the cylindrical coordinates are:
\[ \boxed{\left( 1, \frac{2\pi}{3}, \sqrt{3} \right)} \]

